  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO \nCOUNTIES...\n\nWeaker Santa Ana winds have been observed this morning, mainly below \n35 MPH. This pattern has contributed to very hot temperatures \nthis morning for areas west of the mountains. Valley and coastal \nlocations are close to 95-100 degrees. Subtle increases in onshore\nflow and a weakening of the high pressure system overhead will \nallow increased onshore flow closer to the coast, bringing a few \ndegrees of cooling each day. By Wednesday, confidence starts to \nincrease to see highs below 100 degrees for the valleys and \ncoastal areas of Orange and San Diego Counties. 850mb temperatures\nincrease by Wednesday and Thursday with a thermal ridge poking\ninto the Desert Southwest, leading to an increase in temperatures\nover the mountains and deserts a few degrees from what we have \nseen this weekend. Nights will still remain warm for many areas in\nthe lower to mid 70s for the lower deserts and places west of the\nmountains. Please make sure to be in areas of A/C and check on \nthose without effective cooling. \n\nModels begin to diverge on our next story starting Friday into next \nweekend. Cluster analysis starts to break down the ridge on Friday, \nleading to greater onshore flow and cooling for places from the \nfoothills to the coast. Highs for coastal areas and valleys will \nlower mainly into the 80s and 90s by this time with a better chance \nto see lows below 70 degrees. \n\nAs the ridge weakens, a trough is expected to enter the PacNW around \nFriday or Saturday. Model guidance is continuing to struggle on \nwhere exactly this weather system progresses and how amplified it\nbecomes. A deeper trough across California would lead to more \nsignificant cooling next weekend with some model ensembles showing\nlight amounts of precipitation around Sunday or the following \nMonday (October 12th). Others show the trough staying further \nnorth, which would still bring cooler weather, but keep us on the\ndrier side. Would not be surprised to see a sort of mesh between \nthese depictions to the forecast in the coming days, where cooler \nweather arrives but dry conditions continue.",
